1
00:00:01,485 --> 00:00:06,169
[SPEAKER_01]: How important is thinking in practice?

2
00:00:09,112 --> 00:00:09,813
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not optional.

3
00:00:12,495 --> 00:00:12,876
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

4
00:00:14,197 --> 00:00:14,978
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's not like...

5
00:00:15,578 --> 00:00:24,687
[SPEAKER_00]: I say it's not optional because I know that, you know, people, especially when it comes to the Buddhist practice or even practice of meditation, they think, oh, you just need to stop thinking.

6
00:00:26,881 --> 00:00:31,403
[SPEAKER_00]: And you fail... they fail to understand that that's a contradiction in terms, because that's another thought.

7
00:00:32,383 --> 00:00:36,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Our stop thinking is a thought that just tries to deny itself.

8
00:00:37,865 --> 00:00:40,846
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you go around thinking that you're abstaining from thinking by thinking.

9
00:00:42,466 --> 00:00:43,827
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's impossible to not think.

10
00:00:45,507 --> 00:00:48,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Only in second jhāna, the thoughts would subside.

11
00:00:49,409 --> 00:00:52,350
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not to say that you stopped thinking.

12
00:00:52,410 --> 00:00:56,071
[SPEAKER_00]: It just means you refined your... your... your... your context and your composure,

13
00:00:56,532 --> 00:00:59,473
[SPEAKER_00]: To the extent that even the movement of the thoughts now is too coarse.

14
00:01:01,014 --> 00:01:04,475
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like you should stop thinking.

15
00:01:04,735 --> 00:01:13,518
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about you must learn how to think skillfully, to the point of entering the first jhāna, which is the the purest way of thinking you can have, thinking and pondering.

16
00:01:14,518 --> 00:01:16,479
[SPEAKER_00]: And that, in itself, is enough for Arahantship.

17
00:01:19,015 --> 00:01:20,756
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what about wisdom?

18
00:01:21,156 --> 00:01:29,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people think that wisdom is... or understanding comes from feeling.

19
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:33,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, like the intuitive wisdom.

20
00:01:33,781 --> 00:01:35,202
[SPEAKER_00]: That's another contradiction in terms.

21
00:01:36,062 --> 00:01:37,723
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's intuitive, means it's felt.

22
00:01:38,523 --> 00:01:40,544
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's felt, means it's not understood.

23
00:01:41,585 --> 00:01:43,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the understanding...

24
00:01:43,245 --> 00:01:45,286
[SPEAKER_00]: If you develop understanding, you will be free from feeling.

25
00:01:46,667 --> 00:01:47,307
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said.

26
00:01:47,993 --> 00:01:53,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Arahant, who has developed full understanding of the experience as a whole, he doesn't feel anything as a result of it.

27
00:01:53,338 --> 00:01:54,859
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why he's cooled down.

28
00:01:54,959 --> 00:01:55,800
[SPEAKER_00]: He's in nibbāna.

29
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:57,942
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a person who feels doesn't understand.

30
00:01:58,542 --> 00:01:59,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

31
00:02:00,024 --> 00:02:02,586
[SPEAKER_00]: To the extent you feel, to that extent you're lacking understanding.

32
00:02:03,521 --> 00:02:04,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Or wisdom.

33
00:02:04,941 --> 00:02:06,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's that's what wisdom is.

34
00:02:06,382 --> 00:02:07,242
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that understanding.

35
00:02:07,302 --> 00:02:11,003
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't mean understanding in psychological manner, or this is what's happening to me.

36
00:02:11,023 --> 00:02:13,024
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean understanding the nature of things.

37
00:02:13,064 --> 00:02:13,924
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what wisdom is.

38
00:02:14,724 --> 00:02:15,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Wisdom means perspective.

39
00:02:16,324 --> 00:02:18,005
[SPEAKER_00]: We covered that in other talks, basically.

40
00:02:18,025 --> 00:02:20,826
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean intelligence, doesn't mean... but means perspective.

41
00:02:21,666 --> 00:02:26,047
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't have, oh, intuitive perspective that I can't quite put in the words.

42
00:02:26,087 --> 00:02:27,507
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not perspective.

43
00:02:27,587 --> 00:02:29,448
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just you mystifying your own feelings.

44
00:02:35,138 --> 00:02:45,527
[SPEAKER_01]: So, trying to understand something, a problem, by feeling... By feeling what?

45
00:02:49,370 --> 00:03:03,482
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I've got a problem, so if I feel the problem, then feeling it, experiencing it, through feeling, I will understand it and become wise.

46
00:03:05,197 --> 00:03:06,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Just through mere feeling it.

47
00:03:06,898 --> 00:03:07,439
[SPEAKER_01]: So, feeling it.

48
00:03:07,919 --> 00:03:08,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, why?

49
00:03:08,459 --> 00:03:12,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Then we wouldn't need the whole dhamma and the Buddha's teaching.

50
00:03:12,563 --> 00:03:15,966
[SPEAKER_00]: If all you need to do is feel it, to be free from it.

51
00:03:16,507 --> 00:03:21,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would the Buddha spend 40 years of teaching how to free yourself from feeling and suffering involved with it?

52
00:03:24,214 --> 00:03:26,075
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah.

53
00:03:26,776 --> 00:03:27,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you choose to

54
00:03:29,985 --> 00:03:32,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Feel it more or not, the problem is there first.

55
00:03:32,666 --> 00:03:33,807
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a problem in itself.

56
00:03:33,987 --> 00:03:35,547
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that you're liable to a problem.

57
00:03:36,648 --> 00:03:40,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if there is no problem, as the Buddha said, you recognize.

58
00:03:40,789 --> 00:03:45,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I a subject to sickness, aging, death, misfortune, loss?

59
00:03:46,292 --> 00:03:46,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I am.

60
00:03:47,372 --> 00:03:52,334
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now I'm not sick, dying or losing anything, but I am subjected to it.

61
00:03:52,874 --> 00:03:56,176
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, I am liable to these things.

62
00:03:56,216 --> 00:03:57,236
[SPEAKER_00]: They will have to happen.

63
00:03:59,377 --> 00:03:59,597
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

64
00:04:00,138 --> 00:04:03,420
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't actually want to wait for that problem to become acute.

65
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:10,164
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to recognize it that even without the problem, you are within that liability to a problem.

66
00:04:10,244 --> 00:04:12,165
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what dukkha is.

67
00:04:12,185 --> 00:04:14,626
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha wasn't sick and dying when he left the home.

68
00:04:15,467 --> 00:04:19,089
[SPEAKER_00]: He saw other people being sick and dying and then he said, oh, I'm...

69
00:04:19,309 --> 00:04:20,090
[SPEAKER_00]: I cannot escape this.

70
00:04:20,410 --> 00:04:23,372
[SPEAKER_00]: I will... this will have to affect me at some point in the future.

71
00:04:24,092 --> 00:04:26,153
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the concern that he talks about.

72
00:04:26,533 --> 00:04:28,575
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you wait for the problem to be affecting you,

73
00:04:29,453 --> 00:04:42,084
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, to feel it, that's just you trying to basically wiggle your way out of it, sort of manage it, psychologize it, deal with it, once it has arisen.

74
00:04:43,825 --> 00:04:45,206
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how to get wisdom?

75
00:04:45,526 --> 00:04:54,114
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you get wisdom from... Well, you get wisdom by developing a perspective on your own situation.

76
00:04:54,334 --> 00:04:56,035
[SPEAKER_00]: That perspective is just what I described.

77
00:04:56,055 --> 00:04:56,716
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about

78
00:04:58,581 --> 00:05:02,242
[SPEAKER_00]: When I get a problem, I will do this, and then I will do that, and then I will feel this.

79
00:05:02,762 --> 00:05:09,825
[SPEAKER_00]: All that can be in place, whatever, but you want to extend that perspective and realize, can I not have any problem in the future?

80
00:05:10,785 --> 00:05:18,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Are there things in me that I'm not abandoning, because of which problems can land on me, suffering can affect me?

81
00:05:20,088 --> 00:05:27,231
[SPEAKER_00]: So you actually... that's what wisdom is, preemptively developing perspective to basically leave the domain where the problems arise.

82
00:05:28,259 --> 00:05:29,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandon suffering.

83
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:31,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, dukkha does not mean suffering.

84
00:05:31,521 --> 00:05:32,542
[SPEAKER_00]: It means dis-ease.

85
00:05:32,702 --> 00:05:34,523
[SPEAKER_00]: It means that uneasiness.

86
00:05:35,624 --> 00:05:42,729
[SPEAKER_00]: And you are uneasy when you don't have any problem, but when you recognize that in a day or two, there's going to be a big problem coming your way.

87
00:05:43,029 --> 00:05:46,671
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know what specific problem it will be, but you know it will have to arise.

88
00:05:46,751 --> 00:05:47,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, you get anxious.

89
00:05:47,832 --> 00:05:49,874
[SPEAKER_00]: You get uneasy.

90
00:05:49,894 --> 00:05:54,457
[SPEAKER_00]: See, but there is no problem, specifically, that you have to feel and get in touch with.

91
00:05:55,327 --> 00:05:59,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, there is a problem, because you recognize that liability to a problem is a problem.

92
00:05:59,592 --> 00:06:02,837
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can try and feel it all you want, it will not uproot your liability.

93
00:06:04,899 --> 00:06:14,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Only developing that perspective further and abandoning passion in regard to it, is what develops that wisdom, that uproots the liability to it.

